WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.340
In a human embryo, the brain and spinal cord begin as a flat sheet of cells on the

2
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:15.280
back. By the end of about the fourth week after fertilization, that sheet has rolled

3
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.780
into a closed tube.

4
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.220
This is often before a woman knows she is pregnant.

5
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.780
So what happens if the tube does not close?

6
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.500
The answer starts with distance.

7
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.660
How far apart are the edges of the sheet?

8
00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:32.623
The sheet is the neural plate.

9
00:00:33.443 --> 00:00:38.863
It creases along hinge points, where cells shrink one face and turn wedge-shaped.

10
00:00:39.843 --> 00:00:42.623
The edges rise as two neural folds.

11
00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:47.323
The folds move toward the middle, meet, and fuse.

12
00:00:48.563 --> 00:00:53.083
Then the tube pinches off from the skin, and the skin seals over it.

13
00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:56.253
Why does the width of the plate matter?

14
00:00:57.133 --> 00:01:01.993
Think of two people on opposite sides of a wide table, reaching to shake hands.

15
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:05.513
They can lean, but their arms have a fixed reach.

16
00:01:06.273 --> 00:01:07.593
The folds are the same.

17
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.933
They can rise and move inward only so far.

18
00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:14.293
If they start too far apart, they never meet.

19
00:01:15.013 --> 00:01:18.773
So before the tube can close, the plate must become narrow.

20
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:25.167
Narrowing comes from convergent extension.

21
00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:30.147
Cells in the plate push in between their neighbors on either side.

22
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:33.007
This move is called intercalation.

23
00:01:33.907 --> 00:01:37.507
Each step makes a row one cell narrower and longer.

24
00:01:38.387 --> 00:01:43.147
Many small steps turn a short, wide plate into a long, thin one.

25
00:01:44.147 --> 00:01:48.007
For this to work, the cells must agree on which way is sideways.

26
00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:51.907
That sense of direction is planar cell polarity.

27
00:01:53.047 --> 00:01:57.527
Proteins including Dishevelled gather on opposite sides of each cell.

28
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.980
Frog embryos tested this idea.

29
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.040
The fate of each early frog cell is mapped, so researchers can aim an injection.

30
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:12.540
They put a disrupted Dishevelled into the cells that form the midline of the plate.

31
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.800
The plate stayed wide.

32
00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.840
The folds still rose and moved inward.

33
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.980
But the tube stayed open.

34
00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:26.453
Now aim the disruption at the sides instead, where the folds form and fuse.

35
00:02:27.253 --> 00:02:29.013
This time, closure was normal.

36
00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:34.313
So the signal was needed at the midline, where narrowing sets the distance between

37
00:02:34.313 --> 00:02:37.053
the folds. Mice tell the same story.

38
00:02:37.693 --> 00:02:42.973
In the loop-tail mouse, the midline failed to extend before folding began, and

39
00:02:42.973 --> 00:02:44.193
closure never started.

40
00:02:45.093 --> 00:02:49.733
Keep this rule. If the plate stays too wide, the folds cannot meet.
